    21	# A Quick Start Guide
    22
    23	This guide provides instructions for running the SCX schedulers as a systemd service and checking its logs.
    24
    25	## Getting Started
    26
    27	At the very beginning, configure the /etc/default/scx file:
    28
    29	- in the SCX_SCHEDULER variable, select the scheduler you are interested in
    30
    31	- in the SCX_FLAGS variable, specify the flags you want to add. To do this, execute and read what flags you can add.
    32
    33	```
    34	scx_SCHEDNAME --help
    35	```
    36
    37	To start the SCX scheduler at boot, you need to run the systemd service as root. Here are the steps:
    38
    39
    40	- Enable the service:
    41
    42	```
    43	systemctl enable scx.service
    44	```
    45
    46	- Start the service:
    47
    48	```
    49	systemctl start scx.service
    50	```
    51
    52	Alternatively, you can use a shortened version of these commands:
    53
    54	```
    55	systemctl enable --now scx.service
    56	```
    57
    58	- To check the status of the service, use the following command:
    59
    60	```
    61	systemctl status scx.service
    62	```
    63
    64	## Override global configuration
    65
    66	It is possible to override the global scx settings using systemd environment
    67	variables `SCX_SCHEDULER_OVERRIDE` and `SCX_FLAGS_OVERRIDE`.
    68
    69	Example:
    70
    71	```
    72	systemctl set-environment SCX_SCHEDULER_OVERRIDE=scx_rustland
    73	systemctl set-environment SCX_FLAGS_OVERRIDE="-s 10000"
    74	systemctl restart scx
    75	```
    76
    77	If you want to restore the default value from the `/etc/default/scx` file execute:
    78
    79	```
    80	systemctl unset-environment SCX_SCHEDULER_OVERRIDE
    81	systemctl unset-environment SCX_FLAGS_OVERRIDE
    82	systemctl restart scx
    83	```
    84
    85	## Checking journald Logs
    86
    87
    88	- To view the logs, use the following command:
    89
    90	```
    91	journalctl -u scx.service
    92	```
    93
    94	- To view the logs of the current session use the command
    95
    96	```
    97	journalctl -u scx.service -b 0
    98	```
    99
   100	# Drop-in for scx_loader.service — swap in the AnimaSchedEXT build of
   101	# scx_lavd (the one that pins anima_active_pids for the focus daemon).
   102	#
   103	# Place at /etc/systemd/system/scx_loader.service.d/anima.conf
   104
   105	[Service]
   106	ExecStart=
   107	ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/scx_lavd-anima --performance
   108	Restart=on-failure
   109	# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
   110	#
   111	# AnimaSchedEXT — Hyprland focus daemon (systemd USER service)
   112	#
   113	# Subscribes to Hyprland's activewindowv2 events and pushes the focused
   114	# PID into scx_lavd's pinned anima_active_pids BPF map. LAVD then gives
   115	# that PID the tightest virtual deadline (see anima.bpf.h).
   116	#
   117	# Why a user service (not a system service)?
   118	#   The daemon needs HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR to
   119	#   talk to the Hyprland IPC socket. These env vars are only present in
   120	#   the user's graphical session — a system service can't see them
   121	#   without ugly passthrough hacks. Plus the pins are chmod 666 so no
   122	#   root/CAP_BPF is required to update the maps.
   123	#
   124	# Install:
   125	#   install -Dm644 anima-hypr.service ~/.config/systemd/user/
   126	#   systemctl --user daemon-reload
   127	#   systemctl --user enable --now anima-hypr.service
   128	#
   129	# Logs:
   130	#   journalctl --user -u anima-hypr.service -f
   131
   132	[Unit]
   133	Description=AnimAIOS Hyprland focus daemon (scx_lavd boost)
   134	PartOf=graphical-session.target
   135	After=graphical-session.target
   136
   137	[Service]
   138	Type=simple
   139	# window+tree: boost focused PID + its low-CPU direct children. Switch to
   140	# just "window" if you want the focused process only (no descendants).
   141	ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/anima-hypr-daemon window+tree
   142	Restart=on-failure
   143	RestartSec=3
   144
   145	[Install]
   146	WantedBy=graphical-session.target
   147	# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
   148	#
   149	# tmpfiles.d snippet for AnimaSchedEXT
   150	#
   151	# Creates /run/anima on boot so the user daemon can bind its tab socket
   152	# without needing root. The directory is world-writable so any browser
   153	# native-messaging host (which runs as the user) can connect.
   154	#
   155	# Install:
   156	#   sudo install -Dm644 anima.conf /etc/tmpfiles.d/
   157
   158	d /run/anima 0777 root root -

