     1	# Building schedulers in the `scx` project
     2
     3	The `scx` repository is organized as a **Cargo workspace** containing multiple schedulers, shared libraries, and tools.
     4	Schedulers are implemented as individual Rust crates under `scheds/rust/*`.
     5
     6	This document explains how to build the entire project as well as individual schedulers and tools.
     7
     8	---
     9
    10	## 1. Available build profiles
    11
    12	The project defines several Cargo build profiles in the top-level `Cargo.toml`:
    13
    14	- **release**
    15	  Thin LTO enabled (default for production).
    16
    17	- **release-tiny**
    18	  Stripped, thin LTO, optimized for small binary size.
    19
    20	- **release-fast**
    21	  Optimized for compilation speed and native CPU optimizations, no LTO.
    22
    23	You can select a profile using the `--profile` option, for example:
    24
    25	```bash
    26	cargo build --profile=release-tiny
    27	```
    28
    29	---
    30
    31	## 2. Building the entire workspace
    32
    33	To build **all crates** (schedulers, libraries, and tools):
    34
    35	- Debug build (default):
    36
    37	  ```bash
    38	  cargo build
    39	  ```
    40
    41	- Optimized release build:
    42
    43	  ```bash
    44	  cargo build --release
    45	  ```
    46
    47	- Tiny profile:
    48
    49	  ```bash
    50	  cargo build --profile=release-tiny
    51	  ```
    52
    53	- Fast profile:
    54
    55	  ```bash
    56	  cargo build --profile=release-fast
    57	  ```
    58
    59	---
    60
    61	## 3. Building individual schedulers
    62
    63	Each scheduler is its own Cargo package. You can build a single one using:
    64
    65	```bash
    66	cargo build --profile=<profile> -p <scheduler_name>
    67	```
    68
    69	Example for **scx_flash** with **release-tiny**:
    70
    71	```bash
    72	cargo build --profile=release-tiny -p scx_flash
    73	```
    74
    75	---
    76
    77	### List of schedulers
    78
    79	| Scheduler name | Example build command |
    80	|----------------|------------------------|
    81	| `scx_bpfland`  | `cargo build --release -p scx_bpfland` |
    82	| `scx_chaos`    | `cargo build --release -p scx_chaos` |
    83	| `scx_cosmos`   | `cargo build --release -p scx_cosmos` |
    84	| `scx_flash`    | `cargo build --release -p scx_flash` |
    85	| `scx_lavd`     | `cargo build --release -p scx_lavd` |
    86	| `scx_layered`  | `cargo build --release -p scx_layered` |
    87	| `scx_mitosis`  | `cargo build --release -p scx_mitosis` |
    88	| `scx_p2dq`     | `cargo build --release -p scx_p2dq` |
    89	| `scx_rlfifo`   | `cargo build --release -p scx_rlfifo` |
    90	| `scx_rustland` | `cargo build --release -p scx_rustland` |
    91	| `scx_rusty`    | `cargo build --release -p scx_rusty` |
    92	| `scx_tickless` | `cargo build --release -p scx_tickless` |
    93	| `scx_wd40`     | `cargo build --release -p scx_wd40` |
    94
    95	---
    96
    97	## 4. Building tools
    98
    99	Besides schedulers, the workspace includes several tools:
   100
   101	- **scxtop** – Monitoring tool:
   102
   103	  ```bash
   104	  cargo build --release -p scxtop
   105	  ```
   106
   107	- **scxcash** – Caching utility:
   108
   109	  ```bash
   110	  cargo build --release -p scxcash
   111	  ```
   112
   113	- **vmlinux_docify** – Kernel documentation generator:
   114
   115	  ```bash
   116	  cargo build --release -p vmlinux_docify
   117	  ```
   118
   119	---
   120
   121	## 5. Installing from crates.io
   122
   123	Some schedulers and tools may also be available directly from [crates.io](https://crates.io). This allows you to install them without cloning the repository.
   124
   125	### Examples
   126
   127	| Crate name   | Install command            |
   128	|--------------|----------------------------|
   129	| `scxtop`     | `cargo install scxtop`     |
   130	| `scx_flash`  | `cargo install scx_flash`  |
   131
   132	This will place the binary in `~/.cargo/bin`, which you should add to your `PATH` if it is not already included.
   133
   134	> **Note**: Availability on crates.io depends on which components the maintainers publish there. Not all schedulers may be published.
   135
   136	### Installing system-wide
   137
   138	To make a scheduler or tool available system-wide, you can either:
   139
   140	1. Copy the installed binary from `~/.cargo/bin` into a system directory, e.g.:
   141
   142	   ```bash
   143	   sudo cp ~/.cargo/bin/scxtop /usr/local/bin/
   144	   ```
   145
   146	2. Or add `~/.cargo/bin` to your system `PATH`, for example by adding this line to `~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc`:
   147
   148	   ```bash
   149	   export PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH"
   150	   ```
   151
   152	---
   153
   154	## 6. Running tests
   155
   156	To verify the correctness of the build, you can run tests:
   157
   158	- For the entire workspace:
   159
   160	  ```bash
   161	  cargo test
   162	  ```
   163
   164	- For a specific scheduler:
   165
   166	  ```bash
   167	  cargo test -p scx_flash
   168	  ```
   169
   170	---
   171
   172	## 7. Dependency management
   173
   174	The workspace uses a shared `Cargo.lock` file.
   175
   176	- To prefetch dependencies for offline builds:
   177
   178	  ```bash
   179	  cargo fetch --locked
   180	  ```
   181
   182	- To update dependencies:
   183
   184	  ```bash
   185	  cargo update
   186	  ```
   187
   188	---
   189
   190	## 8. Cross-compilation
   191
   192	You can build for different targets, for example **musl**:
   193
   194	```bash
   195	cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl
   196	```
   197
   198	Make sure the target is installed first:
   199
   200	```bash
   201	rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl
   202	```
   203
   204	---
   205
   206	## 9. Debugging
   207
   208	- Enable backtraces:
   209
   210	  ```bash
   211	  sudo env RUST_BACKTRACE=1 ./target/debug/scx_flash
   212	  ```
   213
   214	- Enable debug logging:
   215
   216	  ```bash
   217	  sudo env RUST_LOG=debug ./target/debug/scx_flash
   218	  ```
   219
   220	---
   221
   222	## 10. Cleaning up
   223
   224	To remove build artifacts and start fresh:
   225
   226	```bash
   227	cargo clean
   228	```
   229
   230	---
   231
   232	## 11. Summary
   233
   234	- **Build everything**: `cargo build --release`
   235	- **Build one scheduler**: `cargo build --profile=<profile> -p <name>`
   236	- **Install from crates.io**: `cargo install <crate_name>`
   237	- **Make available system-wide**: copy binary to `/usr/local/bin` or add `~/.cargo/bin` to `PATH`
   238	- **Run tests**: `cargo test`
   239	- **Cross-compile**: `cargo build --target=<target>`
   240	- **Profiles available**: `release`, `release-tiny`, `release-fast`
   241
   242	This approach allows you to build and test either the whole project at once or focus on a single scheduler or tool.

