     1	<div align=center>
     2
     3	# [Anima](https://github.com/animaios/anima)SchedEXT
     4
     5	<img width="256" height="384" alt="AnimAIOS mascot" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6d6699ed-e61b-4e8a-8148-6d9ab4b36cef" />
     6
     7	[![ sched_ext](https://img.shields.io/badge/sched_ext-BPF_scheduler-orange)](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx)
     8	[![ Hyprland](https://img.shields.io/badge/Hyprland-aware-blueviolet)](https://hyprland.org)
     9	[![ License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-GPL--2.0-blue.svg)](LICENSE)
    10
    11	**AnimaSchedEXT** is a purpose-built fork of the
    12	[scx_lavd](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx/tree/main/scheds/rust/scx_lavd)
    13	(**L**atency-criticality **A**ware **V**irtual **D**eadline) scheduler from the
    14	[sched_ext](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx) project, tuned for the desktop
    15	gamer and interactive power user.
    16
    17	The core LAVD scheduler is already excellent at keeping the system responsive
    18	under load: it classifies tasks by communication behavior and gives the most
    19	urgent, latency-sensitive ones the tightest virtual deadlines. AnimaSchedEXT
    20	adds one piece it was missing — awareness of which window is *actually focused
    21	in your window manager*.
    22
    23	</div>
    24
    25	---
    26
    27	## How it works
    28
    29	```mermaid
    30	graph TD
    31	    H["Hyprland compositor"] --> |event| D["hypr-daemon"]
    32	    D --> |bpf_syscall| M["pinned BPF map"]
    33	    M --> |lookup| S["scx_lavd-anima"]
    34	    S --> |priority| K["LAVD kernel"]
    35	```
    36
    37	Two moving parts:
    38
    39	- **`scx_lavd-anima`** — a patched `scx_lavd` binary that pins an
    40	  `anima_active_pids` BPF map at `/sys/fs/bpf/anima_active_pids`. Loaded in
    41	  place of the usual `scx_lavd` by `scx_loader`.
    42	- **`anima-hypr-daemon`** — a standalone binary that you run as a systemd
    43	  service alongside `scx_loader`. It watches Hyprland focus events and
    44	  writes the focused PID into the pinned map via direct `bpf()` syscalls
    45	  through `libbpf_rs`.
    46
    47	On start, the daemon reads `hyprctl clients -j` once and caches
    48	`address → PID`. Each focus event is resolved from that cache (with a
    49	fallback `hyprctl activewindow -j` for windows opened after daemon start).
    50
    51	The new PID list is then applied directly to the pinned BPF map via
    52	`libbpf_rs::MapHandle::update` / `delete` — no `bpftool` subprocess, no
    53	JSON parsing, no temp file. Updates are *incremental*: only stale keys are
    54	deleted and only new keys inserted, so each focus change costs O(delta)
    55	syscalls regardless of map size. On `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` and on graceful
    56	exit the map gets cleared the same way so no stale boost survives a daemon
    57	stop.
    58
    59	The kernel-side LAVD sees each PID in the map and sets `lat_cri` to the
    60	user-configurable boost level (default 896, LAVD's top LC bucket). The
    61	focused task therefore wins dispatch on the next round without collapsing
    62	the deadline numerator to zero. Boost level is read from the
    63	`anima_config` BPF map, written by the daemon at startup from the
    64	`ANIMA_BOOST` environment variable.
    65
    66	---
    67
    68	## Installation
    69
    70	### Prerequisites
    71
    72	- Linux kernel **6.12+** with `sched_ext` enabled.
    73	- [hypridle](https://github.com/hyprwm/hypridle) or any idle daemon that
    74	  stops the scheduler when the user goes idle (the stock `gnome-idle.target`
    75	  setup works fine).
    76	- Hyprland (obviously).
    77	- `bpftool` v7.1+.
    78	- `cargo` + `libbpf-dev` for building.
    79
    80	### Build
    81
    82	```bash
    83	cd scheds/rust/scx_lavd
    84	# main scheduler binary (replaces /usr/bin/scx_lavd)
    85	cargo build --release
    86	# userspace hyprland focus daemon
    87	cargo build --release --example anima_hypr_daemon
    88	```
    89
    90	Install:
    91
    92	```bash
    93	# Replace the default scx_lavd with our build
    94	sudo install -Dm755 ../../target/release/scx_lavd /usr/local/bin/scx_lavd-anima
    95
    96	# Install the daemon binary
    97	sudo install -Dm755 ../../target/release/examples/anima_hypr_daemon \
    98	                /usr/local/bin/anima-hypr-daemon
    99
   100	# Install unit files
   101	sudo install -Dm644 services/anima-scx-loader.conf \
   102	                /etc/systemd/system/scx_loader.service.d/anima.conf
   103	sudo install -Dm644 services/anima-hypr.service \
   104	                /etc/systemd/system/
   105	sudo systemctl daemon-reload
   106	```
   107
   108	The drop-in `anima-scx-loader.conf` overrides `scx_loader.service` to launch
   109	`scx_lavd-anima` instead of the stock `scx_lavd`. Combined with
   110	`services/anima-hypr.service`, the chain at boot is:
   111
   112	```
   113	systemd ──► scx_loader.service ──► scx_lavd-anima (pins BPF map)
   114	                                       │
   115	               anima-hypr.service ─────┘ (subscribes + updates)
   116	```
   117
   118	Enable everything:
   119
   120	```bash
   121	sudo systemctl enable scx_loader.service
   122	sudo systemctl enable anima-hypr.service
   123	sudo systemctl start scx_loader.service anima-hypr.service
   124	```
   125
   126	### Tuning the boost level
   127
   128	The focused-window boost level is runtime-configurable via the
   129	`ANIMA_BOOST` environment variable. Default is 896 (top 12.5% LC bucket).
   130	Valid range: 0 (disable boost) .. 1024 (=LAVD_SCALE). Values above ~900
   131	start collapsing `dl_delta` toward zero — tune with care.
   132
   133	```bash
   134	# Optional: tune boost at runtime (default 896, max 1024)
   135	ANIMA_BOOST=768 sudo -E /usr/local/bin/anima-hypr-daemon window+tree
   136	```
   137
   138	To make the change permanent, edit `services/anima-hypr.service` and add
   139	`Environment="ANIMA_BOOST=768"` to the `[Service]` section, then
   140	`systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart anima-hypr.service`.
   141
   142	### Filtering heavy children (window+tree mode)
   143
   144	By default, `window+tree` boosts the focused PID plus its direct
   145	children. Some of those children are background compute (a compiler
   146	spawned from a terminal, a video encoder, the compile farm inside an
   147	IDE) — exactly what LAVD is meant to de-prioritize.
   148
   149	The daemon samples each child's `/proc/<pid>/stat` for one second, two
   150	samples apart, and drops any child whose CPU fraction during the
   151	sampling window exceeded `ANIMA_CHILD_MAX_CPU` (default 0.5 = half a
   152	core). Set it to 1.0 to disable filtering entirely.
   153
   154	```bash
   155	# Only boost children using <30% of a core on average
   156	ANIMA_CHILD_MAX_CPU=0.3 sudo -E /usr/local/bin/anima-hypr-daemon window+tree
   157	```
   158
   159	The same environment variable is respected via systemd `Environment=`.
   160
   161	### Runtime
   162
   163	To switch between `scx_lavd` and `scx_lavd-anima` on a running system:
   164
   165	```bash
   166	# Patch in
   167	sudo systemctl stop scx_loader.service
   168	sudo cp target/release/scx_lavd /usr/local/bin/scx_lavd-anima
   169	sudo systemctl start scx_loader.service anima-hypr.service
   170
   171	# Revert
   172	sudo systemctl stop scx_loader.service anima-hypr.service
   173	# restore stock scx_lavd path and restart scx_loader
   174	```
   175
   176	When `anima-hypr-daemon` is running, watch the focus updates:
   177
   178	```bash
   179	$ journalctl -u anima-hypr.service -f
   180	[anima] focused -> 2356175 (with 0 children)
   181	[anima] focused -> 2356843 (with 0 children)
   182	```
   183
   184	And verify the kernel saw the boost:
   185
   186	```bash
   187	$ sudo bpftool map dump pinned /sys/fs/bpf/anima_active_pids
   188	key: 0x0023 f9 6f (value 0x01)   # whatever the focused PID is
   189	```
   190
   191	---
   192
   193	## Configuration
   194
   195	Edit `services/anima-hypr.service`:
   196
   197	- `ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/anima-hypr-daemon window` — boost only the focused window's PID. This is the right default because many GUI apps (terminals, IDEs, etc.) fork children that are background compute — exactly what LAVD is meant to de-prioritize.
   198	- `ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/anima-hypr-daemon window+tree` — also boost direct children of the focused PID. Useful when the focused window forks a renderer/helper process you want kept snappy, but be aware: this will also boost a compiler or encoder running inside. Direct children only, never a deep tree walk.
   199
   200	The stock `scx_lavd` flags still apply to `scx_lavd-anima` — see the upstream
   201	scx_lavd help. The drop-in `anima-scx-loader.conf` currently sets
   202	`--performance`; adjust to `--balanced` or remove the flag to autopilot-mode
   203	match your taste.
   204
   205	---
   206
   207	## How the LAVD scheduler itself works
   208
   209	This section is kept brief — see the upstream
   210	[scx_lavd](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx/tree/main/scheds/rust/scx_lavd)
   211	documentation for the full story. The很短 version:
   212
   213	- Every task gets a **latency criticality** score based on how frequently it
   214	  wakes other tasks and how short its runs are. A task that sits in the middle
   215	  of a chain like *game engine → render thread → compositor* gets a higher
   216	  score than a long-running background compile.
   217	- The scheduler also computes a **virtual deadline** — shorter deadline means
   218	  LAVD picks you sooner. LC score is the denominator of that deadline.
   219	- On `ops.enqueue`, LAVD calls `calc_lat_cri()`. Our patch adds:
   220
   221	  ```c
   222	  if (is_anima_focused_pid((u32)p->pid)) {
   223	      u32 zero = 0;
   224	      u32 *boost = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&anima_config, &zero);
   225	      taskc->lat_cri = boost ? *boost : LAVD_ANIMA_LC_BOOST_DFL;
   226	  }
   227	  ```
   228
   229	  This forces the focused task into LAVD's top LC bucket using the
   230	  runtime-configurable boost level. The daemon writes the boost value into
   231	  the `anima_config` BPF map at startup; if the lookup fails (e.g.,
   232	  scheduler booted before the daemon), the default `LAVD_ANIMA_LC_BOOST_DFL`
   233	  (= 896 = top 12.5% LC) applies. Setting the boost higher divides the
   234	  deadline down to sub-ns granularity (`dl_delta == 0`), which makes the
   235	  focused task's virtual deadline land at
   236	  `cur_logical_CLK - LAVD_DL_COMPETE_WINDOW` — indistinguishable from any
   237	  other starving task. That starves background work without adding useful
   238	  priority separation. 896 (= `LAVD_SCALE * 7 / 8` = LAVD's own
   239	  `LAVD_LC_LATENCY_SENSITIVE_THRESH`) is enough to guarantee dispatch win
   240	  on the next round without collapsing the deadline to zero.
   241
   242	Caveat: preemption is cooperative. If the focused task is blocked, normal
   243	LAVD rules apply to everything else. The boost only affects runnable PIDs, and
   244	only the PID that Hyprland currently considers focused.
   245
   246	---
   247
   248	## Repository structure
   249
   250	```
   251	anima-lavd-fork/
   252	├── scheds/rust/scx_lavd/
   253	│   ├── src/
   254	│   │   ├── bpf/
   255	│   │   │   ├── anima.bpf.h      # pinned BPF map + is_anima_focused_pid
   256	│   │   │   ├── lat_cri.bpf.c    # patched LC calc (boost focused tasks)
   257	│   │   │   └── main.bpf.c       # pin map at /sys/fs/bpf/anima_active_pids
   258	│   │   └── main.rs              # scx_lavd entry, chmod pinned map
   259	│   └── examples/
   260	│       └── anima_hypr_daemon.rs  # standalone Hyprland focus daemon
   261	└── services/
   262	    ├── anima-hypr.service       # systemd unit for the daemon
   263	    └── anima-scx-loader.conf    # drop-in that swaps in scx_lavd-anima
   264	```
   265
   266	### Upstream support
   267
   268	AnimaSchedEXT patches land in two forms:
   269
   270	- The **BPF patch** (everything in `src/bpf/`): merged into sched_ext's
   271	  scx_lavd when the community agrees. Until then, this small fork keeps the
   272	  patches compiling against upstream releases.
   273	- The **focus daemon + systemd units** (everything else): ships in this repo,
   274	  not upstream. The daemon relies on Hyprland-specific IPC that upstream has
   275	  no interest in maintaining.
   276
   277	Both live in the same repo so they share a version number and get tested
   278	together.
   279
   280	---
   281
   282	## License
   283
   284	GPL-2.0 only — same as the upstream scx project. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).
   285
   286	---
   287
   288	## Credits
   289
   290	- [Changwoo Min](https://github.com/changuk-min) and the
   291	  [Igalia](https://igalia.com/) crew for the upstream `scx_lavd` scheduler.
   292	- [sched_ext](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx) maintainers for the BPF
   293	  scheduler framework.
   294	- The Hyprland project for its excellent IPC.
   295
   296	---
   297
   298	## Status
   299
   300	We track the upstream scx repository. Whenever scx releases a new LAVD
   301	feature, we rebase this fork on top and re-do the few-line `is_anima_focused_pid`
   302	patch. Bug fixes specific to the focus-daemon come from the AnimAIOS community.
   303
   304	If you find a bug in this patch (not in the core LAVD logic), please open
   305	*this repo's* issues — the upstream project does not maintain the
   306	Hyprland-specific parts.

