    70	# Statistics transport library for sched_ext schedulers
    71
    72	[`sched_ext`](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx) is a Linux kernel feature
    73	which enables implementing kernel thread schedulers in BPF and dynamically
    74	loading them.
    75
    76	This library provides an easy way to define statistics and access them
    77	through a UNIX domain socket. While this library is developed for SCX
    78	schedulers, it can be used elsewhere as the only baked-in assumption is the
    79	default UNIX domain socket path which can be overridden.
    80
    81	Statistics are defined as structs. A statistics struct can contain the
    82	following fields:
    83
    84	- Numbers - i32, u32, i64, u64, f64.
    85
    86	- Strings.
    87
    88	- Structs containing allowed fields.
    89
    90	- `Vec`s and `BTreeMap`s containing the above.
    91
    92	The following is taken from [`examples/stats_defs.rs.h`](./examples/stats_defs.rs.h):
    93
    94	```rust
    95	#[derive(Clone, Debug, Serialize, Deserialize, Stats)]
    96	#[stat(desc = "domain statistics", _om_prefix="d_", _om_label="domain_name")]
    97	struct DomainStats {
    98	    pub name: String,
    99	    #[stat(desc = "an event counter")]
   100	    pub events: u64,
   101	    #[stat(desc = "a gauge number")]
   102	    pub pressure: f64,
   103	}
   104
   105	#[derive(Clone, Debug, Serialize, Deserialize, Stats)]
   106	#[stat(desc = "cluster statistics", top)]
   107	struct ClusterStats {
   108	    pub name: String,
   109	    #[stat(desc = "update timestamp")]
   110	    pub at: u64,
   111	    #[stat(desc = "some bitmap we want to report", _om_skip)]
   112	    pub bitmap: Vec<u32>,
   113	    #[stat(desc = "domain statistics")]
   114	    pub doms_dict: BTreeMap<usize, DomainStats>,
   115	}
   116	```
   117
   118	`scx_stats_derive::Stats` is the derive macro which generates everything
   119	necessary including the statistics metadata. The `stat` struct and field
   120	attribute allows adding annotations. The following attributes are currently
   121	defined:
   122
   123	*struct and field attributes*
   124
   125	- desc: Description.
   126
   127	*struct-only attributes*
   128
   129	- top: Marks the top-level statistics struct which is reported by default.
   130	  Used by generic tools to find the starting point when processing the
   131	  metadata.
   132
   133	In addition, arbitrary user attributes which start with "_" can be added to
   134	both structs and fields. They are collected into the "user" dict of the
   135	containing struct or field. When the value of such user attribute is not
   136	specified, the string "true" is assigned by default. For example,
   137	[scripts/scxstats_to_openmetrics.py](scripts/scxstats_to_openmetrics.py)
   138	recognizes the following user attribute:
   139
   140	- `_om_prefix`: The value is prefixed to the field name to form the unique
   141	  OpenMetrics metric name.
   142
   143	- `_om_label`: Labels are used to distinguish different members of a dict.
   144	  This field attribute specifies the name of the label for a dict field.
   145
   146	- `_om_skip`: Not all fields might make sense to translate to OpenMetrics.
   147	  This valueless field attribute marks the field to be skipped.
   148
   149	[`examples/stats_defs.rs.h`](./examples/stats_defs.rs.h) shows how the above
   150	attributes can be used. See
   151	[scx_layered](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx/tree/main/scheds/rust/scx_layered/src/stats.rs)
   152	for practical usage.
   153
   154	Note that scx_stats depends on [`serde`](https://crates.io/crates/serde) and
   155	[`serde_json`](https://crates.io/crates/serde_json) and each statistics
   156	struct must derive `Serialize` and `Deserialize`.
   157
   158	The statistics server which serves the above structs through a UNIX domain
   159	socket can be launched as follows:
   160
   161	```rust
   162	    let _server = ScxStatsServer::new()
   163	        .set_path(&path)
   164	        .add_stats_meta(ClusterStats::meta())
   165	        .add_stats_meta(DomainStats::meta())
   166	        .add_stats("top", Box::new(move |_| stats.to_json()))
   167	        .launch()
   168	        .unwrap();
   169	```
   170
   171	The `scx_stats::Meta::meta()` trait function is automatically implemented by
   172	the `scx_stats::Meta` derive macro for each statistics struct. Adding them
   173	to the statistics server allows implementing generic clients which don't
   174	have the definitions of the statistics structs - e.g. to relay the
   175	statistics to another framework such as OpenMetrics.
   176
   177	`top` is the default statistics reported when no specific target is
   178	specified and should always be added to the server. The closure should
   179	return `serde_json::Value`. Note that `scx_stats::ToJson` automatically adds
   180	`.to_json()` to structs which implement both `scx_stats::Meta` and
   181	`serde::Serialize`.
   182
   183	The above will launch the statistics server listening on `@path`. Note that
   184	the server will shutdown when `_server` variable is dropped. The client side
   185	is also simple. Taken from [`examples/client.rs`](./examples/client.rs):
   186
   187	```rust
   188	    let mut client = ScxStatsClient::new().set_path(path).connect(None).unwrap();
   189	```
   190
   191	The above creates a client instance. Let's query the statistics:
   192
   193	```rust
   194	    let resp = client.request::<ClusterStats>("stat", vec![]);
   195	    println!("{:#?}", resp);
   196	```
   197
   198	The above is equivalent to querying the `top` target:
   199
   200	```rust
   201	    println!("\n===== Requesting \"stat\" with \"target\"=\"top\":");
   202	    let resp = client.request::<ClusterStats>("stat", vec![("target".into(), "top".into())]);
   203	    println!("{:#?}", resp);
   204	```
   205
   206	If `("args", BTreeMap<String, String>)` is passed in as a part of the
   207	`@args` vector, the `BTreeMap` will be passed as an argument to the handling
   208	closure on the server side.
   209
   210	When implementing a generic client which does not have access to the
   211	statistics struct definitions, the metadata can come handy:
   212
   213	```rust
   214	    println!("\n===== Requesting \"stats_meta\" but receiving with serde_json::Value:");
   215	    let resp = client.request::<serde_json::Value>("stats_meta", vec![]).unwrap();
   216	    println!("{}", serde_json::to_string_pretty(&resp).unwrap());
   217	```
   218
